==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|woe is I|qq={{glossary|hypercorrect}}}}

===Etymology===
Here, the {{glossary|oblique}} pronoun {{m|en|me}} [[denote]]s not an {{glossary|informal}} {{glossary|copulative}} {{l|en|complement|id=grammar}} (as in ''this is me''), but an {{glossary|indirect object}}, [[wherefore]] it literally means ''woe is to me'', an {{glossary|obsolete}} way of saying ''I feel [[woe]]''; compare {{ncog|de|[[mir]] [[sein|ist]] [[traurig]]||I feel sad|lit=to-me [it] is sad}}. [[idiomatic|Idiomatically]], it means ''woe is upon me''. See also {{ncog|de|weh ist mir}} and its [[descendant#Noun|descendant]], {{ncog|yi|וויי איז מיר}}, which are identical in form and meaning to the English expression.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌwəʊ ɪz ˈmiː/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌwoʊ ɪz ˈmi/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-woe is me.wav|a=US}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{lb|en|sometimes|humorous}} {{ng|Used to show that the speaker feels [[distress]] or [[misery]].}}
